 "result": "**Mechanism:** context-local proxies. Werkzeug `LocalProxy` objects wrap a single `ContextVar`, and each lookup resolves the current object at access time.\n\n**Objects (`src/tessel/globals.py`):**\n- `_app_var` (line 40) is a `ContextVar[AppContext]` named `\"tessel.app_ctx\"`. It holds the current `AppContext`.\n- `app_ctx` (line 41) is a `LocalProxy` to the `AppContext` itself.\n- `active_app` (line 44) is a `LocalProxy(_app_var, \"app\")`, which resolves to `AppContext.app`, the `Tessel` instance.\n- `g` (line 47) is a `LocalProxy(_app_var, \"g\")`, which resolves to `AppContext.g`, the `_AppCtxGlobals` object.\n- `request` (line 57) is a `LocalProxy(_app_var, \"request\")`, which resolves to `AppContext.request`, a `Request` wrapper.\n- `session` (line 60) works the same way as `request`.\n\nAccessing an attribute on a proxy reads `_app_var.get()` and then fetches the named attribute from that `AppContext`. If no context is pushed, the proxy raises the `unbound_message` error (\"Working outside of application context\" or \"Working outside of request context\").\n\nThe type stubs (`TesselProxy`, `RequestProxy`, and so on) are only for type checkers. The `AppContext` class is in `src/tessel/ctx.py`, where the var is set on push and reset on pop. I did not open that file.\n\n`request_ctx` is a deprecated alias for `app_ctx`; the contexts have been merged.",
